  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a multi-scale optimization approach with three distinct stages: global search, resolution adaptation, and local refinement. The core idea is to combine bounded random perturbations with interval analysis to explore the solution space efficiently. By systematically varying the resolution of sampled points, we can (a) maintain population diversity, (b) identify optimal neighborhood configurations, and (c) validate candidates across scales to ensure robustness. This differs from previous approaches by explicitly targeting neighborhood correlations and leveraging interval propagation to enforce constraints.\n    - Global search: Stochastic exploration with adaptive bounds\n    - Resolution adaptation: Dynamic scaling to unmask hidden patterns\n    - Local refinement: Progressive zooming to final configurations\n\nJustification: The problem is high-dimensional with complex constraints. Previous methods often trapped in local minima by focusing exclusively on fine-resolution optimization. Our approach explores mathematical structures across scales to uncover fundamentally improved solutions. The primary limitation is computational pedigree in formal verification, but we can mitigate this through statistical validation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Candidates: Step functions h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with \u222bh dx = 1\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u22641, \u222bh dx = 1\n- Matheuristic framework:\n  1. Initialize h0 = initial_h_values\n  2. Global phase: Randomized search over bounded intervals with dynamic rejection\n  3. Adaptation phase: Coarse-to-fine resolution sweeping\n  4. Refinement phase: Local search guided by interval constraints\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nImplementation protocol:\n1. Initial phases\n- Load initial_h_values if available\n- Set n_points = 700 (default)\n- Directories: (not applicable)\n2. Global search\n- Initialize population P\u2080 of 50 candidates:\n   - Basic candidates: Uniform draw from [0,1] \u00d7 volume\n   - Modified candidates: Draw in relative intervals per neighborhood\n- Selection criteria: Rank fitness (C5) + constraint violation cost\n- For each candidate:\n   - Enforce bounds via projection\n   - Local refinement with adaptive gradient correction\n3. Resolution adaptation\n- Dynamic resolution set: {350, 500, 700, 840, 1000}\n- Transition matrix: Predefined paths \u2192 coarse \u2192 fine\n- Dimension reduction: Piecewise-linear mapping between resolutions\n4. Local refinement\n- Gradient-based search with projection\n- Constraint handling: Interval analysis propagation\n5. Optimization mechanics:\n- evaluate_c5(h): Fast convolution with projected gradient\n- enforce_bounds(h): Bounded projection and centroid adjustment\n- simplex_adaptation(h): Create candidates by resampling in constraints-derived boxes\n6. Termination criteria:\n- Exhaust one resolution sweep\n- Local convergence (gradient stalls)\n- Time ceiling (1100s)\n7. Artifacts:\n- Best_candidate: Most improved across resolutions\n- Final tally: (h_array, c5, n_points)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Dynamic gradient tolerance: Min = 1e-4, linear annealing\n- Adaptive mutation: Neighbors sampling over [low,high] bounds\n- Dimension sensitivity: Suspend dimensions with correlation >0.75\n- Resolution mapping: Logarithmic spacing between coarser and finer\n- Annealing schedule: C5 improvement plateau \u2192 switch resolution\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Core operations:\n  - Global population: 50 \u00d7 10s = 500s/second level\n  - Resolution changes: 350\u2194700 mapping: 5s per transition\n  - Local refinement: 0.8\u00b0CPU\n- Scheduled checkpoints at 20%, 60%, 100% of target time\n- Soft failover: Use median of neighborhood costs\n- Validation protocol: Cross-resolution checking every evaluation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Post-hoc checks: Interval bounds and integral normalization\n- Objective evaluation: Repeated low-cost convolutions\n- Statistical acceptance: Two-sample t-testing for improvement\n- Final output: (h_optimized, c5, n_f) meeting:\n   - Length between 350 and 1000\n   - Integral exact to 1e-6\n   - All values constrained (clip detection)\n\nImplementation laundry list:\n- Use project_feasible with priority constraints\n- Cache correlated coeffF between resolutions\n- Pre-compute \u03c6(idx) = corr vector for quick evaluation\n- Resource guard: Time budget per resolution change is 120s\n- Compatibility: Handle different resolutions via interpolation",
